  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1239 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nSynoptic Overview... \n\nAn upper-level trough is currently situated over the inter-mountain \nwest. Embedded within this parent trough is a shortwave currently \nover the Dakotas, supporting a surface low pressure center in \ncentral South Dakota, with a cold front stretched in central \nNebraska (more to come). The main vorticity maximum associated with \nthis system currently resides near Southeastern Colorado. \n\nThis Afternoon and Evening...\n\nStrong southerly winds, gusting to 35 MPH, are expected to continue \nthrough the afternoon ahead of this approaching cold front. \nFurthermore, skies will continue to clear, as the surface low \npressure center continues to lift northeast. These clear skies \nand strong southerly winds will allow highs to increase into the\n70s ahead of the cold front.\n\nAs this surface low continues to develop underneath the parent \ntrough, a cold front will advance into the area. As this cold front \nbarrels through the area, it will serve as the main forcing \nmechanism for scattered strong to marginally severe thunderstorms \nbetween 3-5 PM, mainly along and southeast of a line from roughly \nPhillipsburg to Aurora to Columbus (though this could shift slightly \ndepending of frontal timing). Strong winds and hail will be the\nprimary threat, as surface dew points over 60 degrees (high for\nearly November) allow CAPE values to reach 1200-1500 J/kg, \nalong with 30-40kts of effective layer shear. However, a couple \nweak, isolated tornadoes are not out of the question, as we have\nroughly 20 kts of 0-1km shear and over low-level SRH values \nover 100. The main timeframe for these severe hazards will be \n4-8PM. These storms could also be efficient rain producers \nthanks to precipitable water values over 1 inch and a long- \nskinny CAPE profile, with localized 2-3 inches possible in \nstrongest cores. \n\nTonight and Tomorrow...\n\nWhile severe storms are not likely after 9PM, isolated thunderstorms\nand rain showers will continue through the night, before \neventually trailing off in the early afternoon. An additional \n0.10-0.25 inches of precipitation are possible with these \nshowers/storms. Even after the precipitation has weaned, the low\npressure center will continue to have its effect on the local \nweather. Strong, gusty winds will also continue through tomorrow\nevening, with gusts up to 40-45 MPH possible. Highs tomorrow \nwill be limited to the mid 40s to mid 50s, as the low-level \nstratus layer prevents much sunshine along with some strong cold\nadvection through the day.\n\nRest of the Week ... \n\nThe rest of the forecast period will see near-\nnormal temperatures, with highs in the 50s, as the upper level low \nhangs out in the Midwest through at least midweek. While most of the \nforecast is expected to be dry, there is a small chance (up to 15%) \nof showers Wednesday night into Thursday morning, in the broad\ncyclonic flow around the upper low as it moves east. After \ntonight, low temperatures will return to to the 30s, with below \nfreezing temperatures possible each night, particularly for \nnorthwestern portions of the area. Friday morning looks to be \nthe coolest, with lows falling below 30 degrees (freezing line \nroughly from Phillipsburg to Hastings to York).",
